WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
How can one fatty acid become roughly 106 ATP?

2
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.540
Watch a 16-carbon chain enter this mitochondrial workshop, then count what leaves.

3
00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:17.170
In its simplest form, beta β-oxidation handles a saturated, even-numbered chain.

4
00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.450
Fat stores many electrons on carbon–hydrogen bonds.

5
00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:26.310
The workshop harvests them while removing two carbons per turn.

6
00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:30.190
First, however, the chain must reach the machinery.

7
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:36.430
During fasting or exercise, fat droplets release fatty acids into blood.

8
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:40.170
Albumin carries them to energy-using tissues.

9
00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:45.930
Activation attaches coenzyme A and spends two ATP equivalents.

10
00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:51.650
Long-chain cargo then crosses the inner membrane through the carnitine shuttle.

11
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.600
Inside the matrix, count from the coenzyme A end.

12
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.620
Carbon 2 is α, and carbon 3 is β.

13
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.200
A plain chain offers no cutting handle.

14
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.400
Three moves rebuild the beta β-carbon, preparing the bond beside it.

15
00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:16.130
First oxidation makes a double bond and reduces FAD to FADH₂.

16
00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.850
Hydration adds water, placing an OH on the beta β-carbon.

17
00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:27.310
Second oxidation makes a ketone and reduces NAD⁺ plus to NADH.

18
00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:29.730
Now thiolase can cut.

19
00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:36.393
Thiolase adds fresh coenzyme A and releases one two-carbon acetyl-CoA.

20
00:01:37.493 --> 00:01:41.813
The remaining chain is two carbons shorter, so it returns to the first step.

21
00:01:43.273 --> 00:01:48.213
Beta β-oxidation converts chain length into a ledger of turns, carrier pairs, and

22
00:01:48.213 --> 00:01:49.553
acetyl coenzyme A. Beta -oxidation converts chain length into a ledger of turns,

23
00:01:49.553 --> 00:01:50.413
carrier pairs, and acetyl coenzyme A.

24
00:01:50.617 --> 00:01:52.377
Predict the palmitate ledger.

25
00:01:53.617 --> 00:01:59.777
A 16-carbon chain becomes how many 2-carbon acetyl-CoA units, after how many turns?

26
00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:03.457
Place your answer beside the empty counters.

27
00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:13.367
Eight acetyl-CoA units leave, but only seven turns run.

28
00:02:14.787 --> 00:02:19.627
Seven cuts make eight pieces, and the last turn releases two acetyl-CoA.

29
00:02:20.947 --> 00:02:26.067
Therefore, 7 FADH₂ and 7 NADH also enter the ledger.

30
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.260
Each carrier pair yields about 4 ATP through oxidative phosphorylation.

31
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:39.320
Each acetyl-CoA yields about 10 through the citric acid cycle and oxidative

32
00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:45.440
phosphorylation. Seven pairs give 28; eight acetyl-CoA give 80.

33
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.280
Subtract the 2-ATP activation toll.

34
00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.040
The result is roughly 106 ATP.

35
00:02:54.333 --> 00:02:59.793
That count applies to a saturated, even chain burned completely to carbon dioxide.

36
00:03:01.173 --> 00:03:03.993
Double bonds and odd chains change the accounting.

37
00:03:05.293 --> 00:03:06.873
The reusable rule remains.

38
00:03:08.293 --> 00:03:11.873
Count the turns, the acetyl-CoA, and the activation toll.
